# a meditation 

🕉️ my head, my hands, my mind, the stones, the sand, the ocean: all part of the unified and connected whole 🕉️ 

Picture a crisp clean white sheet of paper, the most vibrant bleached clear white you have ever seen. The paper is made from wood pulp that has been tirelessly worked over, hand mixed, stirred with a walnut paddle in a copper vat, pressed, dried, cleaned. The paper is white and flawless. You are zoomed in so close you can see the midday sunlight highlighting individual fibers in the thick paper. 

Now picture a sharp pencil, made from cedar, glossy black lacquer deep as midnight on its hexagonal faces, sharpened to a point like a pin. The monk's left hand grasps the pencil, not too tightly, not too loosely. You are still zoomed in. You can see the whorls and ridges on his fingerprint. You can see the sunlight through the skin of his fingertip, glowing dim and red, and you can even detect his pulse just by watching. 

The pencil presses down on the paper, black against white, and you are zoomed in so closely you can see motes of graphite spray into the air and almost invisibly skitter across the paper. The pencil begins to move slowly in a straight line and you see a thick smear of black carbon on the otherwise pristine page. The path is a straight road. You can zoom out and see the line is a `1`. 

The pencil presses down on the paper, white silhouetting the black, and you are zoomed in so closely you can see that the pencil's tip is barely blunted by the drawing of the previous character. It has conformed to the shape and texture of the page. The pencil moves again, this time in an even curve. A pathway of carbon trails off behind the pencil but soon the pencil comes back to the start. The path is a circle. You can zoom out and see the line is a `0`. 

The more you zoom out, the more you see details that surprise you. The monk sits by a sand garden raked in complicated paths, with smooth black stones against the bright bleached sand. The stones spell out `1` and `0` as well. The more you zoom out the more sand gardens you notice, not just in this courtyard but in thousands of courtyards, spread across the landscape, from the seaside up into the mountains. In every courtyard is a monk writing letters. In every courtyard is a garden spelling out his ideas. 

In every courtyard, you are the monk; the code is the dream that all of the monks have when they sleep. The code is the vision that all of the monks are pursuing. Whether you are looking closely or far off, you can see the order and cleanliness. You can see an errant cherry petal that needs to be cleaned and you can see a line or loop being completed by the monk, in the monastery. You are the monk, the monastery, the pencil, the sand, and the stones. 

Seek order by drawing the correct figures; zoom out and perceive the truth of the binary. 